<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>XRay.Office core abstractions: units, geometry, colour, format identification, the document model and the device-independent display list. No external dependencies by design.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    American English hyphenation patterns, shipped so that line breaking matches LibreOffice's
    without a Hunspell installation. Content rather than an embedded resource, for the same
    reason as XRay.Office.Text's bundled faces: it is a file on a search path, and a user who
    supplies French or Spanish patterns drops them in a directory beside these rather than
    rebuilding the assembly. See Globalization/Hyphenators.cs.

    THE README TRAVELS WITH THE DATA AND IS NOT OPTIONAL. hyph_en_US.dic carries no copyright
    comment of its own (five keyword lines and then bare patterns), so its BSD-style notice
    exists only in README_hyph_en_US.txt, which the licence requires be redistributed with it.
    Copying it into the same folder is what makes the package's own terms honest; the rest of
    this project is MPL-2.0 and that file is not.

    Forward slashes: an MSBuild glob written with backslashes does not expand on Linux, and it
    fails silently: the build stays green and the output directory simply has no patterns.
    TargetPath rather than Link, because Link is the IDE's display path while TargetPath is what
    decides where a copied file lands.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="Globalization/Hyphenation/*"
             TargetPath="hyphenation/%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest"
             Pack="true"
             PackagePath="contentFiles/any/any/hyphenation" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
